Background. In standard cosmology, the observed anisotropies of the cosmic microwave background (CMB) are well described by a nearly scale-invariant primordial power spectrum P(k) of curvature fluctuations, conventionally derived from quantum field theory on a curved (typically quasi-de Sitter) spacetime sourced by a hypothetical scalar "inflaton" field. While phenomenologically successful, inflaton-based models are introduced as effective fields and have not been derived from, nor naturally embedded in, a more fundamental theory of quantum gravity.

Problem. Construct a fundamental, quantum-gravity-compatible model — i.e., a model whose underlying degrees of freedom are those of a candidate theory of quantum gravity (for example a discrete or non-perturbative theory of spacetime such as a causal-set, spin-foam, loop, or analogous framework) and which does not postulate an ad hoc inflaton scalar field — that:

(1) predicts, from first principles, a primordial spectrum of quantum fluctuations of geometric/matter degrees of freedom on cosmological scales whose two-point function reproduces the observed nearly scale-invariant CMB temperature and polarization power spectra, including the measured amplitude, spectral tilt, and constraints on running and non-Gaussianity;

(2) provides a precise dynamical mechanism by which these primordial quantum fluctuations decohere/classicalize and seed the growth of large-scale structure consistent with present observations of galaxy clustering and the matter power spectrum;

(3) reduces, in an appropriate semiclassical/continuum limit, to general relativity coupled to the observed matter content, so that the standard ΛCDM evolution after recombination is recovered.

Specify the model, derive the predicted primordial power spectrum and higher-order correlators, and exhibit the structure-formation mechanism, demonstrating compatibility with current cosmological data without invoking a fundamental inflaton field.

